## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about end-to-end exactly-once semantics across HTTP, DB, Kafka, and Redis in CNPA stacks, including failure modes and observability.

## Key Concepts

- Exactly-once across services
- Outbox pattern and transactional boundaries
- Deduplication using client_request_id
- Idempotent sinks/consumers and cache invalidation
- Observability: duplicates, latency, retry rates

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: HTTP handler scaffold
async function handleOrder(req, res) {
  const id = req.headers['client_request_id'];
  if (!id) return res.status(400).send('missing id');
  await db.transaction(async (tx) => {
    await tx.query('INSERT INTO orders (...) VALUES (...);');
    await tx.query('INSERT INTO dedupe (client_request_id) VALUES (?) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;', [id]);
    await tx.query('INSERT INTO outbox (order_id, event) VALUES (...);');
  });
  res.status(200).send({ status: 'accepted' });
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate idempotent consumer behavior under replay scenarios?
- What metrics and alarms would you configure to detect dedupe or latency anomalies?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to reason about idempotency and delivery guarantees in CNPA patterns, at a beginner level, while nudging toward practical implementation details.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotent enrichment
- Exactly-once delivery
- Outbox pattern
- Deduplication cache
- Producer config (idempotence, acks)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: dedup check before processing
async function enrich(event, db, cache) {
  const id = event.event_id;
  if (await cache.has(id)) return; // already processed
  const profile = await db.getProfile(event.user_id);
  const enriched = { ...event, profile };
  await cache.add(id, /* ttl */ 60000);
  await publishToKafka(enriched);
  await writeOutbox(enriched);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test edge cases like cache eviction and outbox failures?
- How would you adapt if events arrive out of order or with duplicate IDs?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes understanding of cache invalidation, versioned keys, outbox patterns, and end-to-end consistency in CNPA stacks. It also checks how to observe impact and plan safe rollouts.

## Key Concepts
- Cache invalidation strategies and TTLs
- Redis key versioning and cache-aside pattern
- Event-driven invalidation via Kafka
- Safe reads with read-fallbacks and refresh
- Observability of latency and hit ratios

## Code Example
```javascript
// Update path
BEGIN;
UPDATE profiles SET name=$1, email=$2, version = version + 1 WHERE id=$id;
COMMIT;
redis.del(`profile:${id}:v*`);
publishKafka('profile_updated', { id, version: NEW_VERSION });
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions when read happens concurrently with update?
- How would you tune TTLs and decide when to prune old keys?

